# Direct runtime dependencies. Edit this, then regenerate:
#
#     pip-compile --generate-hashes --strip-extras \
#       --output-file=requirements.txt requirements.in
#
# Expect this to take a long time -- tens of minutes, not seconds. Hash mode
# fetches the hash of every published file for every resolved package, and
# `cffi`, `pydantic-core`, `uvloop`, `psycopg-binary` and `argon2-cffi-bindings`
# between them publish several hundred wheels each. The slowness is in the
# compiled dependencies, not a network fault; if it is still running after an
# hour, that is the reason and not a hang.
#
# `requirements.txt` is generated and hash-pinned, and is what CI and production
# install. It is committed rather than resolved at install time because a service
# holding student records should install exactly what was reviewed, not whatever
# the index serves that day. The hashes are what make that claim about the
# artifacts rather than only about the version numbers.
fastapi
uvicorn[standard]
sqlalchemy[asyncio]
alembic
psycopg[binary]
pydantic-settings
email-validator

# Symmetric JWT signing, deliberately: the `crypto` extra is not installed.
#
# Asymmetric signing buys key separation between the issuer and the verifier,
# which matters when a third party has to check a token and the issuer must not
# hold the ability to mint them. That is not this service -- it mints and verifies
# its own tokens, so HS256 is sufficient, and key rotation is handled by the
# key set in settings rather than by a new key pair.
#
# The saving is real: `cryptography` is a compiled wheel of a few megabytes that
# would have to be audited and shipped for no capability actually in use.
pyjwt

# Password hashing. Argon2id needs a compiled extension, which was a genuine
# operational cost when this project was designed and is not one now:
# `argon2-cffi-bindings` publishes a single `abi3` wheel covering CPython 3.10
# through 3.15 on manylinux x86_64 and aarch64, macOS arm64, and Windows.
argon2-cffi
